WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.759
Doors burst open, allowing the gurney to come
speeding through, followed by a gaggle of

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.130
concerned Foundation scientists and medical
personnel.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:15.210
Everyone was abuzz, anxious at the troubling
development and fearful over the fate of the

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.850
patient lying on the examination bed.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.470
It wasn’t exactly a rare occurrence for
someone working at an SCP Foundation facility

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:28.119
to need serious medical treatment, more often
because of a grievous injury or psychological

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.630
episode incurred whenever a dangerous anomaly
breached containment.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.560
After all, working for the Foundation was
nothing if not hazardous to your health.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.989
But personnel being injured?

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:43.760
That was so commonplace at the Foundation
that it was practically mundane, just another

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.719
ordinary part of the job.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:50.570
It certainly never garnered this much attention,
with every researcher and medic on the site

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:55.579
all clamoring to try and assist or even just
get a look at who was on the gurney.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:01.010
It wasn’t a member of personnel; it was
an anomaly.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.530
SCP-049 was sick.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.690
Nothing about the Plague Doctor’s behavior
had seemed out of the ordinary, he was normal,

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.390
at least, as much as a beak-masked, robed
creature could be.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.350
Until, that is, one morning SCP-049 had just
collapsed in his holding cell.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.790
The security camera watching him had recorded
the entire ordeal, with guards alerting the

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:26.570
research teams, and eventually calling paramedics
to come and escort the Plague Doctor to the

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.670
on-site infirmary.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.310
SCP-049 was noticeably weaker, confused…

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.930
even afraid, some onlookers seemed to think.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.220
“There’s no sign of physical injury,”
Doctor Cooper noted aloud, trying his best

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.570
to examine SCP-049 while the gurney was racing
down the hallways, and without touching the

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.490
Plague Doctor’s skin directly; one touch
and he’d die in an instant.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.740
“All signs seem to point to something internal.”

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.110
Nobody had entered SCP-49’s cell in the
previous twenty four hours, the security footage

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.430
proved that the only people who had even come
close to the Plague Doctor were authorized

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.110
Foundation staff.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.320
Doctor Cooper was right.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.910
There was no indication that SCP-049 had suffered
any external damage.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.739
If he’d been attacked, it would’ve been
caught by the security footage.

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:20.280
It had to be some kind of illness, the Plague
Doctor was slipping in and out of consciousness;

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.410
he could move and groan slightly, but only
had a few fleeting periods of lucidity.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.010
But if he was ill, what with?

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.810
What was the cause?

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.810
Any symptoms, if there had been any leading
up to his collapse, must have been so subtle

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:41.250
that they had gone completely undetected by
everyone that had recently encountered SCP-049.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.879
And was there a risk of cross-contamination?

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:49.350
Was everyone rushing alongside or following
the speeding gurney now infected also?

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.269
As they reached the infirmary, Foundation
medics were already waiting, having prepped

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:56.239
the area for SCP-049’s arrival.

00:02:56.239 --> 00:03:01.470
Wearing gloves over their hands to avoid lethal
direct skin contact, the medical staff hoisted

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:07.440
SCP-049 onto a bed, just as Doctor Omel arrived,
pushing his way through the crowd of other

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.730
researchers and doctors who had piled into
the sick bay.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.049
“Alright, the show’s over, back up and
give him some room to breathe!”

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:18.959
Omel shouted, before turning to get a closer
look at SCP-049.

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:23.900
“We’ve had a similar situation to this
before, if this goes the same way, then there’s

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:24.900
nothing to worry about.”

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.569
At that moment, SCP-049 briefly became lucid
once more.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:33.069
“049, it’s Doctor Omel, can you hear me?”

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:36.060
“Y-yes, I can,” the Plague Doctor replied
weakly.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.030
“Please, help me, Doctor.”

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:43.099
“Well, I’ll do my best,” Omel said,
trying to keep the beaked surgeon’s spirits

00:03:43.099 --> 00:03:44.099
up.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:47.060
“It’s a real good thing I was here that
time you laid an egg, at least we know what

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.330
to expect this time, right?”

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:55.720
“I’m not… not laying another egg,”
SCP-049 rasped through his beak, his pain

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.430
obvious in his tone.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.430
“What?

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.549
Then what the heck’s going on?”

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:02.939
“It happened,” he answered, “P-pestilence.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:04.750
It’s the Pestilence!”

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.140
before losing consciousness again.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.430
The entire infirmary went deathly silent.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:15.540
The Pestilence, the vaguely defined disease
that SCP-049 spent his life seeking out and

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.990
trying to cure.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:22.660
Now it had infected him, and could potentially
claim his life, unless the Foundation could

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:23.669
help him.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:30.070
There was one big problem: nobody had any
idea exactly what the Pestilence really was…

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.009
except SCP-049.

00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:35.640
The next few hours were a non-stop circus.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.550
An emergency quarantine order was issued,
as everyone that had accompanied the gurney

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.770
had to be screened and tested for any possible
signs of contagion.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:50.009
If the Pestilence had actually managed to
infect SCP-049, there was a high chance it

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:54.420
could spread, especially with so many research
staff and medics present.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:59.500
The infirmary was sealed off, any of the medics
who were ‘cleared’ was issued a hazmat

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.990
suit so they could continue to aid the sick
Plague Doctor.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.550
Although confirming that staff were one hundred
percent clear of the Pestilence wasn’t exactly

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:12.199
straightforward, nobody knew what signs or
symptoms to even look for.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:17.770
Normally, it was only SCP-049 and him alone
that could sense the presence of The Pestilence

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:23.440
in a human being… and his only ‘cure’
was turning them into a zombified instance

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.940
of SCP-049-2.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:30.370
A momentary panic started to spread amongst
the Foundation personnel who had been near

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:37.330
SCP-049, stemming from one idea about what
the Pestilence really was: the Bubonic Plague.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:43.319
It didn’t seem too far of a stretch given
SCP-049’s appearance, resembling the clothing

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:49.810
worn by medieval plague doctors when the Black
Death ravaged most of Europe during the 1300s.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:55.030
Given that the bubonic plague was fatal, once
this paranoid rumor set in amongst the Foundation

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:00.979
staff, some began to break down, believing
they had contracted the deadly medieval infection.

00:06:00.979 --> 00:06:05.610
However, after several hours of additional
screening and testing, nobody on site showed

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.440
even a hint of bubonic plague in their systems.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.120
But, all the while, SCP-049 wasn’t getting
any better.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.440
“We’re still in the dark here,” Doctor
Omel said, his frustration fogging up the

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.030
visor of his hazmat suit.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:22.150
“If The Pestilence isn’t bubonic plague,
then what the hell is it?

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:23.729
And how do we cure it?”

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:28.639
“I’ve been going through the archives,
every interaction that SCP-049 has ever had

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:33.160
that we’ve got recorded, so far there’s
nothing conclusive,” Doctor Cooper replied,

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.210
swiping through various incident reports on
a tablet.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:41.600
All the Foundation had ever truly known of
the Pestilence was based on SCP-049’s own

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.729
testimony.

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:47.949
They were aware it was an insidious infection,
capable of ultimately causing death in a subject

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:49.630
that had contracted it.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:57.190
But anyone that SCP-049 had ever operated
on and turned into an SCP-049-2 zombie hadn’t

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:01.540
previously exhibited any anomalous or even
just unusual symptoms.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:06.310
The Plague Doctor had only ever described
the disease in vague terms, and he alone had

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:07.880
been able to sense it.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.780
“I went back over Doctor Hamm’s notes
after his interview with SCP-049,” Cooper

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:13.780
continued.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.639
“Based on these, it looks like there are
a few working theories about what the Pestilence

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:18.639
really is, but…”

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:19.639
“But what?”

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:20.639
Omel replied.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:21.789
“Come on, out with it!”

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:25.640
“Well, they’re pretty abstract, to say
the least,” the fellow Foundation researcher

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:26.640
admitted.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.740
“The Entity is at the top of his list.”

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.340
“You’re kidding?”

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:31.629
Doctor Omel sighed.

00:07:31.629 --> 00:07:36.849
The Entity was something the Foundation had
long been aware of, but had no way of confirming

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:38.569
if it even existed.

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:44.750
It had been learned about from SCP-5000, a
heavily damaged Absolute Exclusion Harness.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:49.379
The suit’s data logs told of an alternate
timeline wherein the SCP Foundation turned

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:55.030
on humanity and began using various anomalies
to commit a worldwide apocalypse.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:59.720
This had been done, according to the information
recovered from the harness, in order to combat

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:03.659
a being known only as The Entity, or “It”.

00:08:03.659 --> 00:08:08.879
Apparently, this being had managed to latch
on to the human race’s collective subconscious,

00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:12.850
and had been manipulating the entire planet
for some nefarious reason.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:19.180
The Entity was what caused humans to feel
pain, and subsequently fed on that pain.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.740
Although nobody was aware of its intentions,
the Foundation of this SCP-5000 timeline had

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:30.290
deemed it to be so diabolical that they had
resorted to wiping out humanity at all costs,

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.580
to prevent the Entity from ever succeeding.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.019
“I think it’s a theory with legs,” one
of the medics chimed in.

00:08:36.019 --> 00:08:41.060
“It can’t be, it’s so dumb,” Doctor
Omel replied dismissively, scratching his

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:42.060
head.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:43.959
“Exactly, it’s so dumb, but so brilliant!”

00:08:43.959 --> 00:08:44.959
the medic replied.

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:45.959
“No!

00:08:45.959 --> 00:08:48.450
It’s just dumb!”

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:49.450
Omel shouted.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.780
“Doctor Omel’s right, according to the
logs from SCP-5000, the Entity affects everyone,”

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.220
Doctor Cooper pointed out.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:58.770
“But SCP-049 doesn’t attempt to operate
on everyone it meets.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.910
If it was trying to combat the Entity, it
would have to ‘cure’ every human being

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:02.910
alive.”

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.790
“Where are we with treating SCP-049?”

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:06.790
Doctor Omel asked.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:11.279
“The longer we spend yakking and doing nothing,
he could be getting even closer to death!”

00:09:11.279 --> 00:09:15.320
“We’re seeing no chance,” the medic
answered solemnly.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:20.460
The Foundation’s immediate course of action
was to administer SCP-049 with a single dose

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.610
of SCP-500, also known as the Panacea.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:30.300
Coming in the form of a red pill, SCP-500
was known to cure a person of all diseases.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.550
“But, as we all know, Panacea doesn’t
have one consistent rate of effectiveness,”

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.820
the medic explained.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.380
“It can take longer to heal a subject depending
on the disease they’ve been infected with.”

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.380
“So, SCP-500 could be working?”

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.380
Doctor Omel asked.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:50.940
“Like I said, we’ve not detected any change
in SCP-049’s condition,” the medic replied.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:54.170
“It could be that the treatment isn’t
working at all because it can’t isolate

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.420
the Pestilence on the Plague Doctor’s system.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:00.810
As far as SCP-500 might know, there’s nothing
there for it to actually heal.”

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.770
“Great, really that’s just wonderful;
we have a miracle cure that works on any illness,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:08.570
and it’s just not doing what it’s meant
to,” Omel sighed.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:10.079
“What about the next of kin?

00:10:10.079 --> 00:10:11.079
Any luck there?

00:10:11.079 --> 00:10:14.050
He’s notoriously good at evading capture,
believe me, I know.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:18.460
But if we tell him what’s going on-”
“Not my department,” the medic shrugged.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:22.160
“I’d say the fact nobody’s brought him
in yet means that our field agents still haven’t

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.390
found him, but your guess is as good as mine.”

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:29.829
“Oh, very insightful,” Doctor Omel said,
his frustration coming out as sarcasm.

00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:33.390
“Just get the hell out of here and see what
you can do for SCP-049.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.209
And send someone else to give us updates next
time!”

00:10:36.209 --> 00:10:38.550
“Hold on, Omel,” Doctor Cooper interjected.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.820
“Taking a look at Doctor Hamm’s notes,
and I’ve come up with another theory about

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:42.970
the Pestilence here.”

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.360
“Well, what is-”
“It’s death.”

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.740
The room fell quiet.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.740
“Death?

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.240
As in the fundamental law of the universe?

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.760
The entire state of not being alive?”

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.769
Omel exploded with outraged disbelief.

00:10:56.769 --> 00:11:03.710
“Listen, what if specifically dying of natural
causes could itself be the Pestilence.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:07.459
Hamm was pretty old when he first interviewed
SCP-049, right?

00:11:07.459 --> 00:11:12.790
And they had a number of sessions together
until one day, SCP-049 turns on him, senses

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:16.841
the Pestilence, and gives Doctor Hamm the
whole…

00:11:16.841 --> 00:11:17.841
‘treatment’.”

00:11:17.841 --> 00:11:19.620
“He zombified him, I know, you don’t need
to be coy, Cooper.”

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:20.620
“Right.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:26.010
Well, during their very first interview at
Site-19, 049 said he could sense a lot of

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:32.450
death there, but not all that many traces
of the Pestilence,” the researcher explained.

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:38.480
“So, what if SCP-049 had started to realize
the odd little signs of Doctor Hamm aging

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:41.470
– greying hair, wrinkles, the usual suspects.

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:45.260
And instead of waiting for Hamm to die, he
cured him immediately.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:50.660
Here, in an interview, 049 said this:
“Do you wait to remove rotten timbers until

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:52.980
the hall collapses on top of you?

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:53.980
No.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:58.209
You find them, and you pull them out and replace
them with those untouched by rot!

00:11:58.209 --> 00:12:03.520
And most of all, you do not simply mock the
structure because it now looks different to

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.520
you.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:05.520
It is strong!

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.490
It is free of disease.”

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:11.550
“But not everyone he’s killed – or ‘cured’
– has been Hamm’s age.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.029
There’s no way, we would’ve spotted that
pattern,” Omel reasoned.

00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:19.420
“And if he was trying to stop people dying,
he wouldn’t start out his procedure with

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:21.149
that lethal touch of his.”

00:12:21.149 --> 00:12:24.690
“Think about it, though, how does SCP-049
cure people?

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.589
You said it yourself, he zombifies them.

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:27.920
He reanimates them.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.460
We’re all infected to him, everybody dies
after all.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.300
So, he cures that with those horrific resurrections
and thus…”

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:39.600
“Cures death,” a flabbergasted Doctor
Omel finished the sentence.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.660
“So, assuming you’re right and the Pestilence
really is just death itself…

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:49.920
SCP-049 is in the other room, dying from completely
natural causes?”

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.160
“And that might be the one thing even we
can’t fix,” Doctor Cooper nodded solemnly.

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:58.360
“Tell that to the O5 Council,” Omel muttered.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.170
Suddenly, one of the medics – a different
one, as requested – came bursting into the

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:03.170
room.

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:04.370
“Sir, the next of kin!

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:07.600
One of the field agents has recovered SCP-049-J!”

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.399
The Plague Doctor was quickly rushed into
an operating theatre while doctors Omel and

00:13:11.399 --> 00:13:13.639
Cooper watched from beyond a glass screen.

00:13:13.639 --> 00:13:20.980
A shorter version of SCP-049 – his son,
SCP-049-J – was guided into the room.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.070
The little plague doctor timidly approached
its father.

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:25.649
“Are you sure this will work?”

00:13:25.649 --> 00:13:26.670
Docotr Cooper asked.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:31.370
“I read the file on SCP-049-J, apparently,
despite all its claims of being a magical

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.110
wizard doctor, he’s never actually healed
anything.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.200
Made a few conditions even worse, in fact.”

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.810
“I was around when Junior over there was
born,” Doctor Omel said.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.980
“You know what SCP-049 said to me when his
egg hatched?”

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:43.980
“What?”

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.070
“He is the cure.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:47.209
That’s how I know this is gonna work…”

00:13:47.209 --> 00:13:52.410
Now go and check out “SCP-049 - BOILED,
SCRAMBLED AND FRIED - PLAGUE DOCTOR TALE”

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.110
and “A DAY IN THE LIFE OF SCP-049”!

